WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.552
Use this checkpoint to connect requirements with concepts.

00:00:03.552 --> 00:00:06.957
Large lake files suggest distributed scanning.

00:00:06.957 --> 00:00:09.777
Grouping by a key may create a shuffle.

00:00:09.777 --> 00:00:16.012
Previous-event analysis suggests LAG. A reusable curated output may be stored as a Delta table.

00:00:16.012 --> 00:00:17.962
Plan inspection begins with EXPLAIN.

00:00:17.962 --> 00:00:23.132
These mappings help you choose the right Spark SQL pattern.

00:00:23.132 --> 00:00:26.652
For this activity, make a prediction before you run anything.

00:00:26.652 --> 00:00:32.132
State what one output row should represent, which records you expect to remain,

00:00:32.132 --> 00:00:37.737
and what edge case is most likely to expose a mistake.

00:00:37.737 --> 00:00:41.602
Then run the SQL and compare the result with that prediction.

00:00:41.602 --> 00:00:48.257
If it does not match, debug the logic in small pieces instead of changing several clauses at once.

00:00:48.257 --> 00:00:52.287
This is the same habit you would use in a real analytics project:

00:00:52.287 --> 00:00:56.800
validate the meaning of the result, not only whether the statement executes.
